        <h2 class="animal-name">snow lepord</h2>
        <p class="detail">Type: big cat</p>
        <p class="detail">Lives in: the cold, high mountains of Central and South Asia,
             including countries such as China, Mongolia, India, Nepal, and Pakistan. It makes its home 
             on steep rocky cliffs, snowy slopes, and alpine meadows, usually at heights of 3,000 to 5,500 
             metres above sea level. These areas have freezing winters, cool summers, and very little rainfall.
              Snow leopards are well adapted to this harsh environment with thick fur to keep them warm, wide 
              furry paws that help them walk on snow, and long tails that provide balance on rocky cliffs and 
              extra warmth. They hunt mountain animals such as wild sheep, goats, marmots, and hares, and their
               spotted grey coat helps them blend into the rocks and snow, making them difficult to see.</p>
                 <p class="detail">A snow leopard is a carnivore, which means it eats meat. Its diet 
                mainly consists of wild mountain animals such as blue sheep, ibex, Himalayan tahr,
                 and argali sheep. It also hunts smaller animals like marmots, pikas, hares, and 
                 birds. Snow leopards are strong and patient hunters, using their powerful legs to 
                 leap onto prey from rocky cliffs. If wild prey is scarce, they may also hunt
                  livestock such as sheep or goats. A snow leopard can eat a large amount of 
                  meat at one time and may return to the same kill over several days until it
                   is finished.</p>
                   <p class="cool-facts">
🐆 Snow leopards are called the "ghosts of the mountains" because they are so quiet and hard to spot.<br>
🦘 They can jump up to 15 metres (50 feet) in a single leap—that's about the length of a school bus!<br>
🧣 Their long, fluffy tails can grow to almost 1 metre (3 feet) long and help them balance on steep cliffs. They also wrap their tails around themselves like a scarf to stay warm.<br>
❄️ Their thick fur keeps them warm in temperatures as low as -40°C (-40°F).<br>
👣 Their wide, furry paws act like natural snowshoes, helping them walk across deep snow.<br>
😺 Unlike most big cats, snow leopards cannot roar. Instead, they make sounds like meows, growls, and a friendly "chuff."<br>
🌙 They are most active at dawn and dusk, when they go hunting.<br>
👶 Baby snow leopards are called cubs, and they stay with their mother for up to two years while learning to hunt.<br>
🏔️ Snow leopards live higher up in the mountains than almost any other big cat.<br>
🌍 There are only an estimated 4,000–6,500 snow leopards left in the wild, making them a vulnerable species.<br>
